=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
The boundary router **r11** is the OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution point between the two halves of this network: the OSPF domain (20.x and other legacy prefixes, anchored at r1) and the EIGRP domain (120.x side, anchored at r12/r13). r11's FRR configuration shows one-way redistribution only: `redistribute eigrp` is present inside `router ospf`, so EIGRP-learned routes (120.x, 102.x loopbacks, 103.x, 113.x) are correctly injected into OSPF and appear in r1's routing table. However, the EIGRP process on r11 has **no `redistribute ospf` statement**, meaning OSPF-originated routes (20.x, 10.x, 30.x, and all other OSPF prefixes) are never injected into EIGRP AS 100. As a result, the EIGRP topology tables on r12 and r13 contain zero OSPF-side prefixes — the two domains are routing-protocol islands. Basic pings happen to succeed only because r12 and r13 carry static kernel default routes (distance 0) pointing back to r11, which then forwards onward via OSPF; this masks the full connectivity loss but leaves the EIGRP domain completely blind to OSPF topology. The root cause is the missing `redistribute ospf` directive inside `router eigrp 100` on r11, almost certainly removed during last night's maintenance. The corrective fix is to add `redistribute ospf` into r11's EIGRP process via `vtysh -c "conf t" -c "router eigrp 100" -c " redistribute ospf"`, which will propagate all OSPF prefixes into the EIGRP domain and restore proper bidirectional redistribution at the routing boundary.
=== END ===